  "articleSummary": "The article argues that hiring AI engineers requires evaluating real judgment and observable evidence rather than polished demos or résumés. It advises defining the specific problem first, using a short paid working session with real tasks, and probing candidates on failure handling, evaluation methods, and system boundaries—not trivia about attention heads. Key technologies mentioned include agent evaluations, idempotency keys, and tool-call traces. Written in an analytical, cautionary tone for technical managers or founders, the piece frames hiring as a structured risk assessment, with recurring metaphors like “open the hood” and “silence is expensive.”",
